      "id": 1,
      "text": "Homer was renamed from Barneyville in 1834 after settlers from Homer, New York, who wanted the village to carry their former hometown’s name.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Homer%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Pioneers began settling the area in 1832, and the village officially incorporated in 1871, marking a long history of community growth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9,
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://homermichigan.org/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Milton Barney opened the first store, sawmill, and hotel on the Kalamazoo River in 1832, laying the economic foundation for Homer.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Homer%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The original Homer mills burned in 1886, a fire that reshaped the town’s industrial landscape.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Homer%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Homer High School’s baseball team set a national record with 75 straight wins from 2004 to 2005, a proud moment for the village.",
